up double digits, with the newest likely-voter surveys near +20", "Garrity remains poorly defined, with reports that a quarter of GOP voters do not recognize her", "All six September expert ratings place the race at Safe or Solid Democratic"], "rationale": "Polls show Shapiro leading by roughly 20 points, including a fresh high-quality likely-voter survey at +20, while he outspends Garrity by more than twenty to one and every major rater calls the race Safe or Solid Democratic. Pennsylvania's near-even partisan lean and the midterm environment argue for an expected margin somewhat below the polling average, but nowhere near competitive.", "watch": "A late surge of Republican outside spending or a national midterm shift toward the GOP could narrow the margin in the final weeks.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Josh Shapiro (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Josh Shapiro seeks a second term as Pennsylvania governor against Republican Stacy Garrity, with Libertarian Ken Krawchuk also on the ballot. Pennsylvania sits near even in partisan lean, but Shapiro holds the advantages of incumbency and a commanding financial edge, and every major race rating published in September places the contest in the Safe or Solid Democratic column.\n\nPolling since spring has shown Shapiro ahead by double digits throughout, with the newest large likely-voter surveys putting the gap near 20 points. He has outspent Garrity by more than twenty to one in broadcast and digital advertising, and reports indicate a quarter of Republican voters do not recognize their own nominee. Prediction markets price Shapiro's chances near 97 percent. The main uncertainty is whether the midterm environment narrows the gap in the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9929, "margin": 15.66}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9928, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9978, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9956, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-2